Rem ==================================================================
Rem sinq.shoddy — Shoddy INtegrated Query
Rem
Rem The query words the library did not already have, and NOTHING else.
Rem Sum, Average, Maximum, Minimum, Median and Freq stay in
Rem stats.shoddy; Map, Filter, Fold, Sort, Range, Reverse and Concat
Rem stay builtins; Any, All, CountIf, Contains, Taken, DropN, Flatten,
Rem Zip and Pair stay in seq.shoddy. A query is written out of THOSE
Rem words plus these, and this machine re-declares none of them.
Rem
Rem     Let top  = OrderByDesc(Filter(orders, IsLarge), OrderValue)
Rem     Let bill = Sum(Map(top, OrderValue))
Rem
Rem WHAT WAS MISSING. The one query verb the library lacked is "by":
Rem every existing sequence word takes a predicate or nothing.
Rem
Rem   1. ordering by a KEY. Sort takes no selector and refuses a record
Rem      outright — "SORT expects all NUMBERs or all STRINGs".
Rem   2. ordering by SEVERAL keys, stably, so ties break in order.
Rem   3. grouping into GROUPS. stats' Freq gives key -> count; nothing
Rem      gave key -> the items themselves.
Rem   4. joining two sequences on a key.
Rem   5. Distinct, Union, Intersect, Except.
Rem   6. walking a record graph that holds itself.
Rem
Rem THE SEQUENCE COMES FIRST in every word here, so the language's own
Rem pipeline form reads as a method chain with no syntax added:
Rem
Rem     cs
Rem         SelectMany(Orders)
Rem         Filter(Fn(o) => OrderValue(o) >= 100)
Rem         OrderByDesc(OrderValue)
Rem
Rem NAMING. Shoddy has one flat word namespace — Include splices names
Rem in, so a machine's helpers are exactly as public as its headline
Rem words and there is no such thing as a private Def. The words a
Rem caller is meant to type are named plainly; everything that is only
Rem scaffolding is prefixed Snq, so the surface to learn is the
Rem unprefixed list and nothing more. The prefix is Snq and not Sq
Rem because seedseq.shoddy already owns thirty-one Sq names, and a
Rem reader should not have to work out which machine SqLength came from.
Rem A caller who has a clashing name of their own takes the machine
Rem under a namespace instead: Include "sinq.shoddy" As Q, then QOrderBy.
Rem
Rem ---- how this machine is written, and why it is not obvious --------
Rem
Rem TWO RUNTIME TRAPS shape nearly every word below. Both were measured,
Rem not assumed, and a natural-looking implementation hits both.
Rem
Rem TRAP 1: REST COPIES. A Shoddy list is contiguous, so Rest hands back
Rem a COPY of the tail — walking n elements with First/Rest copies about
Rem n^2/2 of them. Measured: 12500 elements in 74 ms, 50000 in 3585 ms.
Rem Nothing here walks a list that way. Words that must visit every
Rem element either Fold (a builtin loop) or ToArray once and index with
Rem Nth, which is O(1) on an array.
Rem
Rem TRAP 2: ONLY SELF-TAIL-RECURSION BECOMES A LOOP. A recursive call
Rem that is not the last thing a word does costs a live stack frame per
Rem element, and the ceiling is about 20000 frames — a hard process
Rem kill, not an Error, with no line number. MUTUAL recursion is not
Rem optimised either, so the obvious tidy shape (a chain of small Defs
Rem that hands back to the first) is exactly as fatal as the untidy one.
Rem Every recursive word below therefore calls ITSELF, in tail position,
Rem carrying an accumulator that Reverse puts right at the end. Helper
Rem Defs are used freely, but only where they do not close a cycle.
Rem
Rem The one deliberate exception is the sort's divide-and-conquer, which
Rem is mutually recursive between SnqSortSpan and SnqSortHalves. That is
Rem safe because its depth is log2(n) — seventeen frames for a hundred
Rem thousand elements — not n. Depth, not shape, is what matters there.
Rem
Rem COST, SAID OUT LOUD. Ordering is O(n log n). GroupBy is O(n *
Rem groups); JoinOn and GroupJoinOn are O(n * m); Distinct and the set
Rem words are O(n^2) — all for the reason dict.shoddy is an association
Rem list: there is no hashing here. That is the right trade at the scale
Rem this library is written for, and it is said here so nobody has to
Rem measure to find out.
Rem
Rem EVERYTHING IS EAGER. There is no deferred execution and no query
Rem object, because OrderBy, GroupBy and JoinOn must see every element
Rem before they can yield the first, so a lazy pipeline could not stream
Rem through the operators that matter anyway. FindOpt, TakeWhile and
Rem Choose are the words that stop early, and they stop early by
Rem indexing, not by being lazy.
Rem
Rem Built on seq.shoddy, and on the Option the language predeclares.
Rem ==================================================================

Include "seq.shoddy"

Rem ---- ordering: comparers ----------------------------------------------
Rem
Rem A COMPARER is [ t t -- Number ]: negative if the first sorts first,
Rem zero if neither does, positive otherwise. Ordering is built on
Rem comparers rather than on key selectors alone because that is what
Rem lets ThenBy exist at all. C# hangs tie-breaks off an
Rem IOrderedEnumerable that OrderBy hands back; here the comparer is an
Rem ordinary value, so ThenBy composes two of them and no such type is
Rem needed.

Rem The ordering under every comparer. Works on what < works on —
Rem Numbers and Strings — which is exactly the set of things that can be
Rem a sort key.
Def Compare(a As t, b As t) As Number
    If a < b Then
        0 - 1
    Else
        SnqOver(a, b)

Def SnqOver(a As t, b As t) As Number
    If b < a Then
        1
    Else
        0

Rem A selector becomes a comparer: ByKey(Age) orders by age.
Def ByKey(keyOf As [ t -- u ]) As [ t t -- Number ]
    Fn(a, b) => Compare(Call(keyOf, a), Call(keyOf, b))

Def Descending(cmp As [ t t -- Number ]) As [ t t -- Number ]
    Fn(a, b) => 0 - Call(cmp, a, b)

Rem The tie-break. ThenBy(ByKey(Dept), ByKey(Name)) is one comparer that
Rem consults the second only where the first sees no difference.
Def ThenBy(lead As [ t t -- Number ], tie As [ t t -- Number ]) As [ t t -- Number ]
    Fn(a, b) => SnqTie(Call(lead, a, b), tie, a, b)

Def SnqTie(r As Number, tie As [ t t -- Number ], a As t, b As t) As Number
    If r <> 0 Then
        r
    Else
        Call(tie, a, b)

Rem ---- ordering: the sort ------------------------------------------------
Rem
Rem Merge sort over an ARRAY, because Rest copies (trap 1) and because
Rem Nth is O(1) on an array. A tie takes from the LEFT run, which is
Rem what makes it stable, which is what makes ThenBy mean anything — so
Rem the comparison below is deliberately strict (< 0, never <= 0).
Rem
Rem The merge itself does not recurse at all: it is a Fold over the
Rem output positions, threading Pair(i, acc) where i is how far into the
Rem left run we are. The right-hand index is not carried because it can
Rem be recovered — after k-1 elements have been emitted, j is k - i + 1.

Def SnqTakeLeft(st As Pair, a As Array Of t) As Pair
    Pair(Fst(st) + 1, Prepend(Nth(a, Fst(st)), Snd(st)))

Def SnqTakeRight(st As Pair, b As Array Of t, k As Number) As Pair
    Pair(Fst(st), Prepend(Nth(b, k - Fst(st) + 1), Snd(st)))

Def SnqMergeStep(st As Pair, k As Number, a As Array Of t, b As Array Of t, cmp As [ t t -- Number ]) As Pair
    If Fst(st) > Length(a) Then
        SnqTakeRight(st, b, k)
    Else
        SnqMergeRight(st, k, a, b, cmp)

Def SnqMergeRight(st As Pair, k As Number, a As Array Of t, b As Array Of t, cmp As [ t t -- Number ]) As Pair
    If k - Fst(st) + 1 > Length(b) Then
        SnqTakeLeft(st, a)
    Else
        SnqMergePick(st, k, a, b, cmp)

Def SnqMergePick(st As Pair, k As Number, a As Array Of t, b As Array Of t, cmp As [ t t -- Number ]) As Pair
    If Call(cmp, Nth(b, k - Fst(st) + 1), Nth(a, Fst(st))) < 0 Then
        SnqTakeRight(st, b, k)
    Else
        SnqTakeLeft(st, a)

Rem Every rung of the sort passes ARRAYS, so there is one conversion at
Rem the door and one at the end rather than two per level of the
Rem recursion.
Def SnqMergeRuns(a As Array Of t, b As Array Of t, cmp As [ t t -- Number ]) As Array Of t
    ToArray(Reverse(Snd(Fold(Range(1, Length(a) + Length(b)), Pair(1, { }), Fn(st, k) => SnqMergeStep(st, k, a, b, cmp)))))

Rem The divide half. Mutually recursive with SnqSortHalves, and safe only
Rem because the depth is log2(n): see the header.
Def SnqSortSpan(arr As Array Of t, lo As Number, hi As Number, cmp As [ t t -- Number ]) As Array Of t
    If hi <= lo Then
        SnqSpanEnd(arr, lo, hi)
    Else
        SnqSortHalves(arr, lo, hi, Floor((lo + hi) / 2), cmp)

Def SnqSpanEnd(arr As Array Of t, lo As Number, hi As Number) As Array Of t
    If hi < lo Then
        ToArray({ })
    Else
        ToArray({ Nth(arr, lo) })

Def SnqSortHalves(arr As Array Of t, lo As Number, hi As Number, mid As Number, cmp As [ t t -- Number ]) As Array Of t
    SnqMergeRuns(SnqSortSpan(arr, lo, mid, cmp), SnqSortSpan(arr, mid + 1, hi, cmp), cmp)

Rem Answers a LIST whatever it was handed, unlike Sort, which keeps the
Rem input's kind. Ordering has to buffer the whole sequence anyway, and
Rem one answer shape is easier to reason about than two.
Def OrderWith(xs As List Of t, cmp As [ t t -- Number ]) As List Of t
    ToList(SnqSortSpan(ToArray(xs), 1, Length(xs), cmp))

Def OrderBy(xs As List Of t, keyOf As [ t -- u ]) As List Of t
    OrderWith(xs, ByKey(keyOf))

Def OrderByDesc(xs As List Of t, keyOf As [ t -- u ]) As List Of t
    OrderWith(xs, Descending(ByKey(keyOf)))

Rem ---- the extremes by a key ---------------------------------------------
Rem stats' Minimum and Maximum answer the smallest NUMBER in a list.
Rem These answer the ITEM whose key is smallest, which is a different
Rem question and the one a query usually asks. Ties go to the earliest,
Rem matching the sort. The Opt twin is the real word and the aborting
Rem one is written over it, so there is one set of rules and not two.

Def SnqLowerOf(b As t, x As t, keyOf As [ t -- u ]) As Option
    If Call(keyOf, x) < Call(keyOf, b) Then
        Some(x)
    Else
        Some(b)

Def SnqLower(o As Option, x As t, keyOf As [ t -- u ]) As Option
    Select Case o
        Case Some(b)
            SnqLowerOf(b, x, keyOf)
        Case None
            Some(x)

Def MinByOpt(xs As List Of t, keyOf As [ t -- u ]) As Option
    Fold(xs, None(), Fn(o, x) => SnqLower(o, x, keyOf))

Def MinBy(xs As List Of t, keyOf As [ t -- u ]) As t
    Select Case MinByOpt(xs, keyOf)
        Case Some(v)
            v
        Case None
            Error("MINBY: EMPTY SEQUENCE")

Def SnqHigherOf(b As t, x As t, keyOf As [ t -- u ]) As Option
    If Call(keyOf, b) < Call(keyOf, x) Then
        Some(x)
    Else
        Some(b)

Def SnqHigher(o As Option, x As t, keyOf As [ t -- u ]) As Option
    Select Case o
        Case Some(b)
            SnqHigherOf(b, x, keyOf)
        Case None
            Some(x)

Def MaxByOpt(xs As List Of t, keyOf As [ t -- u ]) As Option
    Fold(xs, None(), Fn(o, x) => SnqHigher(o, x, keyOf))

Def MaxBy(xs As List Of t, keyOf As [ t -- u ]) As t
    Select Case MaxByOpt(xs, keyOf)
        Case Some(v)
            v
        Case None
            Error("MAXBY: EMPTY SEQUENCE")

Rem ---- grouping -----------------------------------------------------------
Rem Groups come back in FIRST-APPEARANCE order and their items in SOURCE
Rem order, which is what C# guarantees and what makes a grouped report
Rem reproducible run to run.

Type Group
    GKey   As u
    GItems As List Of t

Def SnqGroupAdd(g As Group, k As u, x As t) As Group
    If GKey(g) = k Then
        With(g, GItems = Prepend(x, GItems(g)))
    Else
        g

Def SnqGroupBump(gs As List Of Group, k As u, x As t) As List Of Group
    If Any(gs, Fn(g) => GKey(g) = k) Then
        Map(gs, Fn(g) => SnqGroupAdd(g, k, x))
    Else
        Append(gs, Group(k, { x }))

Rem Items were pushed on the front for speed; put them back in order.
Def SnqGroupTidy(g As Group) As Group
    With(g, GItems = Reverse(GItems(g)))

Def GroupBy(xs As List Of t, keyOf As [ t -- u ]) As List Of Group
    Map(Fold(xs, { }, Fn(gs, x) => SnqGroupBump(gs, Call(keyOf, x), x)), SnqGroupTidy)

Rem ---- joining -------------------------------------------------------------
Rem The name Join belongs to str.shoddy, which joins strings with a
Rem separator. These read better beside their two key selectors anyway.

Def SnqMatching(ys As List Of u, xk As k, yKeyOf As [ u -- k ]) As List Of u
    Filter(ys, Fn(y) => Call(yKeyOf, y) = xk)

Def SnqMatched(x As t, ys As List Of u, xk As k, yKeyOf As [ u -- k ], pick As [ t u -- v ]) As List Of v
    Map(SnqMatching(ys, xk, yKeyOf), Fn(y) => Call(pick, x, y))

Rem Inner join: one result per matching pair, in left order, dropping
Rem unmatched elements from both sides.
Def JoinOn(xs As List Of t, ys As List Of u, xKeyOf As [ t -- k ], yKeyOf As [ u -- k ], pick As [ t u -- v ]) As List Of v
    SelectMany(xs, Fn(x) => SnqMatched(x, ys, Call(xKeyOf, x), yKeyOf, pick))

Rem Group join: one result per LEFT element, handed the whole list of
Rem its matches — empty where there are none. This is the left outer
Rem join, and the reason JoinOn needs no OrDefault twin.
Def GroupJoinOn(xs As List Of t, ys As List Of u, xKeyOf As [ t -- k ], yKeyOf As [ u -- k ], pick As [ t List Of u -- v ]) As List Of v
    Map(xs, Fn(x) => Call(pick, x, SnqMatching(ys, Call(xKeyOf, x), yKeyOf)))

Rem ---- distinct and sets ---------------------------------------------------
Rem Records compare STRUCTURALLY, so Distinct means what it should on a
Rem record. LISTS compare by identity — two separately built lists of
Rem the same items are not equal — so a sequence OF LISTS is the one
Rem shape these words cannot speak about. Use DistinctBy with a key that
Rem is a Number or a String when that comes up.

Def SnqPushNew(acc As List Of t, x As t) As List Of t
    If Contains(acc, x) Then
        acc
    Else
        Prepend(x, acc)

Def Distinct(xs As List Of t) As List Of t
    Reverse(Fold(xs, { }, SnqPushNew))

Def SnqPushNewBy(acc As Pair, x As t, k As u) As Pair
    If Contains(Fst(acc), k) Then
        acc
    Else
        Pair(Prepend(k, Fst(acc)), Prepend(x, Snd(acc)))

Rem First one wins, so the survivor of a clash is the earliest. The Pair
Rem carries the keys seen and the items kept, side by side.
Def DistinctBy(xs As List Of t, keyOf As [ t -- u ]) As List Of t
    Reverse(Snd(Fold(xs, Pair({ }, { }), Fn(acc, x) => SnqPushNewBy(acc, x, Call(keyOf, x)))))

Def Union(xs As List Of t, ys As List Of t) As List Of t
    Distinct(Concat(xs, ys))

Def Intersect(xs As List Of t, ys As List Of t) As List Of t
    Distinct(Filter(xs, Fn(x) => Contains(ys, x)))

Def Except(xs As List Of t, ys As List Of t) As List Of t
    Distinct(Filter(xs, Fn(x) => Not Contains(ys, x)))

Rem ---- walking a graph ------------------------------------------------------
Rem SelectMany is the one-to-many hop, and Descendants is that hop
Rem applied to a type that holds itself.
Rem
Rem NOTHING MUTATES IN SHODDY, so a record cannot be made to refer to
Rem itself — there is no way to tie the knot after construction — and a
Rem graph of records is therefore always a finite tree. That is why
Rem there is no cycle check here and none is needed.
Rem
Rem THE ONE CEILING: Descendants recurses into the tree and cannot be
Rem made tail-recursive by an accumulator, so it costs a frame per level
Rem of DEPTH. Depth, not size — a million-node tree twelve deep is fine,
Rem and only a tree deeper than about twenty thousand is not. Nothing
Rem built from records reaches that by accident.

Def SelectMany(xs As List Of t, f As [ t -- List Of u ]) As List Of u
    Flatten(Map(xs, f))

Rem Compose two selectors into one deep read: Thru(Home, City) reads the
Rem city of a person's address.
Def Thru(f As [ t -- u ], g As [ u -- v ]) As [ t -- v ]
    Fn(x) => Call(g, Call(f, x))

Rem Root first, then each child's whole subtree, depth first.
Def Descendants(root As t, kidsOf As [ t -- List Of t ]) As List Of t
    Prepend(root, SelectMany(Call(kidsOf, root), Fn(k) => Descendants(k, kidsOf)))

Rem Every descendant EXCEPT the root, for the common "the parts under
Rem this one" question.
Def Offspring(root As t, kidsOf As [ t -- List Of t ]) As List Of t
    SelectMany(Call(kidsOf, root), Fn(k) => Descendants(k, kidsOf))

Rem ---- navigating where a step can fail --------------------------------------
Rem A graph whose hops are partial — a sum type, an Option field, a
Rem lookup that may miss — is walked with these. Option has no accessor
Rem word by design, so Select Case is the only way in, and these are
Rem where that is written once instead of at every hop.

Def OptMap(o As Option, f As [ t -- u ]) As Option
    Select Case o
        Case Some(v)
            Some(Call(f, v))
        Case None
            None()

Def OptBind(o As Option, f As [ t -- Option ]) As Option
    Select Case o
        Case Some(v)
            Call(f, v)
        Case None
            None()

Def OptOr(o As Option, dflt As t) As t
    Select Case o
        Case Some(v)
            v
        Case None
            dflt

Rem Thru for steps that can fail: stops at the first None instead of
Rem aborting.
Def ThruOpt(f As [ t -- Option ], g As [ u -- Option ]) As [ t -- Option ]
    Fn(x) => OptBind(Call(f, x), g)

Def SnqKeepSome(acc As List Of u, o As Option) As List Of u
    Select Case o
        Case Some(v)
            Prepend(v, acc)
        Case None
            acc

Rem Map and Filter in one pass: keep only the elements f had an answer
Rem for, unwrapped. The word for "parse each of these, discard what did
Rem not parse".
Def Choose(xs As List Of t, f As [ t -- Option ]) As List Of u
    Reverse(Fold(xs, { }, Fn(acc, x) => SnqKeepSome(acc, Call(f, x))))

Rem ---- slicing ----------------------------------------------------------------
Rem seq.shoddy has Taken and DropN for a COUNT. These stop on a
Rem CONDITION, which nothing had. All three index an array rather than
Rem walking with Rest, and recurse into themselves rather than into a
Rem helper: see the header's two traps.

Def SnqSpanOf(arr As Array Of t, lo As Number, hi As Number) As List Of t
    Map(Range(lo, hi), Fn(i) => Nth(arr, i))

Def SnqTakeWhileAt(arr As Array Of t, k As Number, p As [ t -- Boolean ], acc As List Of t) As List Of t
    If k > Length(arr) Then
        Reverse(acc)
    Else
        If Call(p, Nth(arr, k)) Then
            SnqTakeWhileAt(arr, k + 1, p, Prepend(Nth(arr, k), acc))
        Else
            Reverse(acc)

Def TakeWhile(xs As List Of t, p As [ t -- Boolean ]) As List Of t
    SnqTakeWhileAt(ToArray(xs), 1, p, { })

Def SnqSkipWhileAt(arr As Array Of t, k As Number, p As [ t -- Boolean ]) As List Of t
    If k > Length(arr) Then
        { }
    Else
        If Call(p, Nth(arr, k)) Then
            SnqSkipWhileAt(arr, k + 1, p)
        Else
            SnqSpanOf(arr, k, Length(arr))

Def SkipWhile(xs As List Of t, p As [ t -- Boolean ]) As List Of t
    SnqSkipWhileAt(ToArray(xs), 1, p)

Def SnqChunkAt(arr As Array Of t, k As Number, n As Number, acc As List Of List Of t) As List Of List Of t
    If k > Length(arr) Then
        Reverse(acc)
    Else
        SnqChunkAt(arr, k + n, n, Prepend(SnqSpanOf(arr, k, Min(k + n - 1, Length(arr))), acc))

Rem Fixed-size batches, the last one short. A size below one would never
Rem consume the sequence, so it is refused rather than hung.
Def Chunk(xs As List Of t, n As Number) As List Of List Of t
    If n < 1 Then
        Error("CHUNK: SIZE MUST BE AT LEAST 1")
    Else
        SnqChunkAt(ToArray(xs), 1, n, { })

Rem ---- the total twins of First and Last ---------------------------------------
Rem First is a builtin and aborts on an empty sequence; seq's Last does
Rem the same. These say "not there" in the language's own words instead,
Rem which is what a query over a filtered list usually needs.

Def FirstOpt(xs As List Of t) As Option
    If IsEmpty(xs) Then
        None()
    Else
        Some(First(xs))

Def LastOpt(xs As List Of t) As Option
    If IsEmpty(xs) Then
        None()
    Else
        Some(Last(xs))

Rem Some only when there is EXACTLY one — the query that expects a
Rem unique answer and wants to be told when it did not get one.
Def SingleOpt(xs As List Of t) As Option
    If Length(xs) = 1 Then
        Some(First(xs))
    Else
        None()

Def SnqFindAt(arr As Array Of t, k As Number, p As [ t -- Boolean ]) As Option
    If k > Length(arr) Then
        None()
    Else
        If Call(p, Nth(arr, k)) Then
            Some(Nth(arr, k))
        Else
            SnqFindAt(arr, k + 1, p)

Rem The first element passing p, without visiting the rest. This is the
Rem short-circuit a lazy pipeline would have bought, written out.
Def FindOpt(xs As List Of t, p As [ t -- Boolean ]) As Option
    SnqFindAt(ToArray(xs), 1, p)
